Say what is actually true when a scan finds nothing, and cut the query budget

WHY "STILL INDEXING" NEVER CLEARED

The check was photoCount > 0 && seedFaces == 0, which is also true when a
member simply is not in the photos, and when faces were never indexed at
all. Three very different situations, one message, and it never went away.

Scans now read the face records in R2 and report which case it is: empty,
not-processed, no-faces, indexing, or no-match. Each has a different answer
and a different person who can act on it. Your "test" collection turns out
to be fully indexed, 72 faces across 17 photos, 0 pending, so the honest
verdict there is no-match, not indexing.

NEW OPERATOR TOOLS

  GET  /api/collections/{c}/status    photos, processed, withFaces, indexed, pending
  POST /api/collections/{c}/reindex   rebuilds the index from the descriptors in R2
  POST /api/collections/{c}/selftest  takes an indexed face, searches with it, and
                                      checks it finds its own photo

The self-test matters most: it separates "the search is broken" from "this
person is not in these photos", which are indistinguishable from outside and
which is exactly where we were stuck.

MEMBERS CAN BROWSE THE WHOLE COLLECTION

Previously a member saw only their own matches, so an empty scan looked like
an empty collection. There is now a Browse all control, and the empty state
offers it.

QUERY BUDGET

Measured against the live service rather than the mock, a scan was issuing
roughly 640 queries, taking 44 seconds and pushing Vectorize into returning
500s. Rounds and frontier are reduced to bring that to about 148.

Dropping the shard count to 1 was tried and reverted: it made each probe one
query but took overall recall from 100 percent to 72 and profile recall from
100 to 23, because the seed round then truncates. The seed is only
SHARD_COUNT queries in total, so it was never the expensive part.

